is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b08dd7-9fc6-5ce8-8e27-e7014f291bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{221}}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502431} {:answer/id #uuid "4b21e142-3047-56b7-a237-3b2a0a58d269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{11}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502466} {:answer/id #uuid "c04ce841-fd4c-586f-938d-e2c5c79a0af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502509} {:answer/id #uuid "df861d4c-ebbe-53e1-8a68-09595e06c63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{21}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502520} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9bf36b-b824-5f16-9b57-ce151c2c9439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{221}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502525}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502431} :answer-field/id #uuid "b74da730-b26f-5448-9072-0d0d4ba40d91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ef9eaebf-4374-50cb-8736-d5ba873c8cc7" :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed due north-east and $\\mid a|= 10$, while the vector $b$ is directed due south-east and $\\mid b|= 11$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=10^2+11^2\\\\\n&=100+121\\\\\n&=221\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{221}=\\sqrt{221}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{221}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18bdccfd-a872-5628-96d0-c6a02b107d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{22}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502440} {:answer/id #uuid "58542225-bd55-57fc-9250-29f88cdd5797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502473} {:answer/id #uuid "746a7848-54f9-53b2-a59c-3dfee6644d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502482} {:answer/id #uuid "97a315e2-aab2-5fd7-8089-a4d938b54333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{244}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502495} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2195bf-fc5f-5287-9b03-3994142c41cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{61}}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502497} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f044e6-29b1-57c6-8eb2-22eae3836c28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f51ffb2c-3881-5972-b74f-4dee263fdce4" :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed due north-east and $\\mid a|= 10$, while the vector $b$ is directed due south-east and $\\mid b|= 12$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b$. The given directions are perpendicular, so the triangle law of addition gives a right triangle.\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+b|^2&=|a|^2+|b|^2\\\\\n&=10^2+12^2\\\\\n&=100+144\\\\\n&=244\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{244}=2 \\sqrt{61}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u&=\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{61}}(a+b)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc3fc2e-8291-595d-9efe-37b54c54ec70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507628} {:answer/id #uuid "21b545b6-d2ce-5675-b1f3-a77567c762a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507636} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd6bc83-ee8d-5527-acf0-39f4c02d1429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507789} {:answer/id #uuid "8f99aa38-d301-56e1-857e-d573e65c98ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507801} {:answer/id #uuid "acdc3a68-84af-5610-9099-7c787b10a4a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507789} :answer-field/id #uuid "44b72334-231e-5d57-bdc0-d2fb9585f9c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "462f5046-e4ee-5f94-8bfd-00082400022c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74956" :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1}=\\frac{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1)(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1) & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}+1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1}+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c9e982-2244-5a23-aac9-ea8f242dfd59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507626} {:answer/id #uuid "2c45373f-5f36-5270-9c27-ce7558fa56c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507653} {:answer/id #uuid "59f30dc1-5f5d-5fdc-a739-06dad6b23c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507728} {:answer/id #uuid "5beae258-194a-5396-abb9-d22777307972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507731} {:answer/id #uuid "824d43de-2c6c-5256-ab7d-e1e81ca546b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507731} :answer-field/id #uuid "f962e375-138e-5ba3-a11f-61cf507ac9c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6b2bedc8-52f9-5d40-afcd-8617235afd0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129401" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (1,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(1,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (1,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (1,0)$ along the path $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,0)\\,\\,\\,x=1}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161e6fd0-e925-55fa-8d09-6a65d107f751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509728} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3d1a48-5c55-52da-b7e7-fd31db815995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,-4,-4⟩+t\\,⟨0,2,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509817} {:answer/id #uuid "63801628-2a57-5777-861f-90cd5a83b242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509895} {:answer/id #uuid "77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509940} {:answer/id #uuid "af75210d-d1a7-57de-a250-0369260017a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,-4,-4⟩+t\\,⟨2,2,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510063}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509940} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885" :question/math-academy-id "q-131125" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=4-4z$ at the point $(0,2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =4-4z \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,2,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)k \\\\  & =2x\\,i-2y\\,j+4\\,k \\\\ ∇f(0,2,2) & =2(0)\\,i-2(2)\\text{ }j+4\\,k \\\\  & =-4\\,j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,2,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,2,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,2,2)=⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f1e83f-e91e-5090-bc9c-c0d8a5b24dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+\\operatorname{sin}⁡w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509699} {:answer/id #uuid "30cff47c-46a9-5910-9362-18ed14cc8d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509792} {:answer/id #uuid "47299991-0c88-52f8-adac-83f8c4229c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509831} {:answer/id #uuid "87f9ca78-e7eb-5c39-bc73-ffb1b08282b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509983} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a8b398-e764-5a3f-9020-8353ddf15a9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{sin}^{2}⁡u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509983} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d0df2e4-d380-5912-bac6-465760a259a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30a2be53-ad1e-54e7-8cc2-2924faaf8e17" :question/math-academy-id "q-161795" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+\\operatorname{cos}⁡w \\\\ v \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡u+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+\\operatorname{cos}⁡w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v,\\,z(u,v,w)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡w.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aae3ac3-02f2-5d2e-bd85-9eee8e2b917d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=t,\\text{ }y=t,\\text{ }z=\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509950} {:answer/id #uuid "84d505d2-2d10-57ad-9f74-f1753c66adec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-t,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509979} {:answer/id #uuid "ad368c2c-26d6-548c-b643-eedd79a18a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=t,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510059} {:answer/id #uuid "f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=0,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510207} {:answer/id #uuid "f43507f5-6454-586e-8980-28bd1f31c21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=0,\\text{ }y=t,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+2t\\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510210}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510207} :answer-field/id #uuid "c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-37231" :question/problem "Find parametric equations for the normal line to the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=1$ at the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e}).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}).$ Then,  our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e})$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,\\sqrt{e}).$  \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,\\sqrt{e}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,\\sqrt{e}) & =\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{e}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe parametric equations for the normal line to $f(x,y,z)$ at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ are given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={x}_{0}+{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t, \\\\ y & ={y}_{0}+{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t, \\\\ z & ={z}_{0}+{f}_{z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametic equations  for the normal line to our surface that  passes through the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e})$ can be written as \n \n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=0,\\,z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65eac0db-4498-5219-a599-16571ff5b0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509900} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ee05e3-c0ef-572d-b71a-0521af7c7c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510151} {:answer/id #uuid "e13473ff-e770-5bc2-84cc-d2f5a63a463b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510166} {:answer/id #uuid "ec060073-eee0-5eb4-a23b-64f1baba3d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510197} {:answer/id #uuid "f40d8dc6-4b68-5931-af1a-b12969faf5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509900} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d304d66-3b35-5b7a-a10d-e4c98f3bf8ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db419926-6089-5205-b99f-0938320c28f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161799" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+v\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +w\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3v \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $\\theta $ is a fixed constant." :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+v\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +w\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y(u,v,w)=3v,\\,z(u,v,w)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201fe908-79f1-5c96-8882-603760dea14f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513619} {:answer/id #uuid "3f49c5b4-7cf8-5c1b-9bb1-15cfaacad0b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513700} {:answer/id #uuid "45b310bb-a17f-5c57-88ef-88eaeb7ca1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513719} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb3285b-1658-5c85-a2b4-99659e2beceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513831} {:answer/id #uuid "92bbd547-69c8-54a5-adf2-467fb7d18b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513913}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513913} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e47ae0-7d67-5cf0-9030-1e73b5fe057a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc4d5cf2-890e-5aaa-adb5-f60e0888df55" :question/math-academy-id "q-71405" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,k \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz))\\text{ }j+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+(-(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0))\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd0dcd1-823c-5bfe-a3b4-87f697bc1cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/88/88d6157b6d961bfcfe66f9d0635c7c75e754fa33d182971f3e8722821995a7bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518772} {:answer/id #uuid "3522f0ef-1ff6-5054-b459-1bc1a99959f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e2/e23ab97241e392652578b744558f27abf17a6f10887b4532bfd9198a0168d6de.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518778} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0ad3fe-aab5-5c80-a718-10250bc0a44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0f/0f22b1314960b7ddc22461df115e3cc8b883f463e39e0717f494486c9145c461.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518791} {:answer/id #uuid "7500cd0d-e37b-5f4b-928d-4f02799db350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8b/8bdff7dd1b78b495112bb64fcf8c7f88eaa5434924c248654afb645f9146db17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518794} {:answer/id #uuid "935a7585-1072-568f-8a0c-50849d049cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/00/00fae4ff8c65eb9be03ec51e68ddf89e03122c366feefa1cc2f370d2fea8b789.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518794} :answer-field/id #uuid "07507bc4-4f53-51a3-a550-08f3dc917fb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "014ab122-2134-517b-b9a9-d71b60e66dee" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-2 x y\\,i-x^{2}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,\\frac{3}{2}),(-2,- \\frac{3}{2}),$ and $(-2,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-2 x y\\,i-x^{2}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-1,\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\left((-2)(-1)\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=3\\,i-j \\\\ F(-2,- \\frac{3}{2}) & =\\left((-2)(-2)\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-6\\,i-4\\,j \\\\ F(-2,\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\left((-2)(-2)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/8b/8bdff7dd1b78b495112bb64fcf8c7f88eaa5434924c248654afb645f9146db17.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a304352-c54f-51ef-8906-32ee9cdc8b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6c/6c75e8fe5ad41fd863eca4152c172bbba3084eba38b6e54c96f9e084669279ca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518783} {:answer/id #uuid "67f43eb9-dbc2-5820-b523-2f926f10b9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e8/e829b1ebe911b3c3ae1a6e871d10a36931fef7f950933abda5ea226fd4c9ae66.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518790} {:answer/id #uuid "6f17cf54-8843-57c9-a3d9-0c5e55c95936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/09/09fee256ed52affac40d46982cdb3b2e3957677e81ec0aa3266d9885be9e1add.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518792} {:answer/id #uuid "773a8b98-66de-5e5e-b398-a5e44ac67b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5d/5decd9cc7f7088571cf3eecf9f50c7ec87e2f6182df1fbc4b55caaf8bc3e73d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518795} {:answer/id #uuid "d2fa96a1-5147-5de1-b5da-a48e24f11b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/26/267f2a39c365664697da7256d49767c2018b3f63d94a6f55e90b566cc554a27e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518790} :answer-field/id #uuid "d55fcac1-f13e-5aca-8efd-d6b03f32a8f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1b8d00f8-3412-52c9-9322-b2ebe7eaa245" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,\\frac{1}{2}),(-2,- \\frac{3}{2}),$ and $(2,- \\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\left((2)(2)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=2\\,i-8\\,j \\\\ F(-2,- \\frac{3}{2}) & =\\left((2)(-2)\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=6\\,i-8\\,j \\\\ F(2,- \\frac{3}{2}) & =\\left((2)(2)\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-6\\,i-8\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/e8/e829b1ebe911b3c3ae1a6e871d10a36931fef7f950933abda5ea226fd4c9ae66.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0deeee54-5137-5ac3-b9b4-0f091bb47502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/07/07235837dbd839f3603359295ecae29383f96956b0d5724fab8fcfb053dbfd46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518768} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1efae7-38d6-5363-b86d-fd79f952e5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8e/8e3a8f6c8b863dd68bc426baf82151476a347d231f87a763199288b93cdf585c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518801} {:answer/id #uuid "923fd78a-1c49-58e4-81a3-5a3d262a0a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/61/61fff51ff54e097ae5a320948e16d1f93f1bbc1c5a170206b016dbcca1cb4fa5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518807} {:answer/id #uuid "b66cc1d0-bd1d-535d-be46-13f0c13305cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8f/8f967f2dccd9a990c7781f25d8d362a1ac26ad1af9bd0f0600a81f0d868fe377.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518819} {:answer/id #uuid "dce8d86e-80d6-5bd9-ba07-826f7939c84b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/7e/7ec5f0301acbff4f08a5390a9c62504ac91ad78fbf02e55db293e1cd5d05af36.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518825}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518801} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f41bc97-1a45-5ac6-b387-80f11d2c9da4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "25249ebb-67f9-5eb6-a85d-bb04853f2c73" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,- \\frac{5}{4}),(1,- \\frac{3}{4}),$ and $(1,\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,- \\frac{5}{4}) & =\\left((-1)(-2)\\left(- \\frac{5}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-8\\,j \\\\ F(1,- \\frac{3}{4}) & =\\left((-1)(1)\\left(- \\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=\\frac{3}{4}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(1,\\frac{1}{4}) & =\\left((-1)(1)\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/8e/8e3a8f6c8b863dd68bc426baf82151476a347d231f87a763199288b93cdf585c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20376de9-73cc-5220-b1b5-d05e47b251ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/34/34fe0d8361633cbf6a7242584878f75edce90dbf4a353e74c4f3ecb5ff3540b3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518773} {:answer/id #uuid "2325b410-0c6c-5f57-bcd5-530841a89169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0d/0d2feddba8eb9c880f7a0dfd88b2b1f936f8bfa1de33f205c408fd776a33a579.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518774} {:answer/id #uuid "530c6a59-3daa-5170-80e7-2ad2212a0376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3f/3f6994b60e754eea32fa80912a8f97d52489cf40afeb1ed8302c735114c435fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518785} {:answer/id #uuid "8f54eaf6-17e9-532e-9a19-52e471db13d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/06/06e549a074498c1965de40b8e169788d1f17b345eb4d0b6ac0f3448f5ea0d836.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518802} {:answer/id #uuid "922d439f-5c84-51e8-8470-495005da2288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/00/0078b7d156dd5970e03f8397336ec07658f1b314a6d7da856c20dfba0dcebd8c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518774} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba38c6cb-2e18-5cec-833d-5f3d7771d213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4806298d-deed-5b7f-9dfb-a11102a6c5d3" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-x^{2}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,\\frac{3}{2}),(1,- \\frac{1}{2}),$ and $(-1,\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-x^{2}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\left((2)(-2)\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-6\\,i-4\\,j \\\\ F(1,- \\frac{1}{2}) & =\\left((2)(1)\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-i-j \\\\ F(-1,\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\left((2)(-1)\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-3\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/0d/0d2feddba8eb9c880f7a0dfd88b2b1f936f8bfa1de33f205c408fd776a33a579.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f24afde-307e-5a68-9ef6-d776f89a190a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cc/ccaa50fb2c18d3f4f320dfebfaa34522a32a50b8fa0c4bea641f158367716437.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518769} {:answer/id #uuid "279db652-c9e6-5660-a30a-c3664e796959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f1/f1825366a93acf5c83c83973d9689d919e5e4a27a3db7fe31bca95afe0dfef78.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518776} {:answer/id #uuid "4792412b-7e7a-5172-9926-7e53c39b704f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2c/2cdf71a7faea6652cca5c0b87916f6a6ce2f622dfb5a668b386a5c1bf2567d23.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518780} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb11845-5440-5f8e-ab38-09080147e291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/86/866186b6f6f18cca7832b0da458fbd08f01a6cb7d618f2a904f50885eb2e3f0f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518788} {:answer/id #uuid "a099f06e-ecd1-50f5-8536-1f98184ff8e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f6/f686e6a5920875ee373ced37fa6fcac941dc3fbd54489f16d4f27c3ff996b2e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518788} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b11a11-c4c5-5c9c-a8c5-b16ef6212efd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "664de91a-fae2-5c4e-bcb3-71dd355525c5" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-2 x y\\,i-x^{2}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,\\frac{5}{4}),(-2,- \\frac{5}{4}),$ and $(-2,\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-2 x y\\,i-x^{2}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-1,\\frac{5}{4}) & =\\left((-2)(-1)\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-j \\\\ F(-2,- \\frac{5}{4}) & =\\left((-2)(-2)\\left(- \\frac{5}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-5\\,i-4\\,j \\\\ F(-2,\\frac{3}{4}) & =\\left((-2)(-2)\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=3\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/86/866186b6f6f18cca7832b0da458fbd08f01a6cb7d618f2a904f50885eb2e3f0f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73c47d7b-b24d-5cf2-b0c2-6d7f0849e04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/72/72166eb3343bf0855703e987cae9bc13f3f086b9c5ed12b0d122fc895625dbb0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518793} {:answer/id #uuid "ac2d5692-6775-5b7d-ab4e-b438291a6bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/22/22f6819ea214f9f4e28ecf9dcc15a809aeb8e518a2f9c819fcb3c55f92372b77.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518816} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0b44c4-099e-5594-a966-9e9a34f749ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/07/07355ad0b1ad48f062e97eeab30d1d9fbc708c418e232782710d2a69610585aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518824} {:answer/id #uuid "dd179ccb-32c0-5484-a19d-839444ebf6a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cb/cbc69fd8f2bb28a37354e093bdd060dcedfe53c05d137b42636ce0f58f94c39f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518826} {:answer/id #uuid "e3545f29-5add-536f-b92d-21489fef7b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/37/37f3a9ac37226466e1283970c046c9355a2b0d04b4e4da0d1a4bf76281089a5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518826} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b45470e-ab58-5a1e-a081-760df0c3d2cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7c1d0e34-2030-5028-b732-5be6a57c42b4" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,- \\frac{1}{4}),(-1,\\frac{3}{4}),$ and $(-2,- \\frac{3}{4}).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,- \\frac{1}{4}) & =\\left((1)(-2)\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-8\\,j \\\\ F(-1,\\frac{3}{4}) & =\\left((1)(-1)\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(-1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-\\frac{3}{4}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(-2,- \\frac{3}{4}) & =\\left((1)(-2)\\left(- \\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-8\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/cb/cbc69fd8f2bb28a37354e093bdd060dcedfe53c05d137b42636ce0f58f94c39f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c36fff4-0414-5e42-b3f7-1bf6513b3861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4b/4be763c92f457c2436f4b3ef6824ddb151c9c4cb1c948da8bb2615a5be0266b4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518766} {:answer/id #uuid "4de6c361-9c35-5623-986e-37c8f14621b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/40/4060e215b7c065030ef48fb633dc81216de738ebdd9cd6a9fdff6c30886cb071.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518784} {:answer/id #uuid "578f1762-b52d-5429-9715-426aab770bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/72/72186906f29a435fb3dbb9f09d133a2343d6baea068f2ed543147e0edaf66759.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518787} {:answer/id #uuid "91d57135-0f5a-55de-8677-5cbed001fbe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3e/3e7f1d15693dbf9f069d84a6664a7d7808c70fee3a579009487c01ef4d0f7aec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518804} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b57fa5-a012-5ba5-9613-62a1d7c22793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/25/250170b98f74972b6d63a4b62cf1b5ac9cfe3fc3137231c5cdabeee61d4696f6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518815}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #ed